WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Thirteen and a half billion years ago, the first stars ignited across the dark universe.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.500
Light from that cosmic dawn has traveled across expanding space to reach us today.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.000
For the first time, the James Webb Space Telescope captures those earliest galaxies in infrared.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:29.500
Every point of light in this deep field is an entire galaxy, revealing structures never seen before.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Spectroscopic instruments split starlight into chemical signatures: oxygen, hydrogen, and neon.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:52.000
Unfolding a six-point-five-meter beryllium mirror required three hundred single-point precision mechanisms.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:04.000
Shielded by five ultra-thin Kapton layers, the optical bench operates near forty kelvins.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:13.000
Stationed at Lagrange Point Two, one million miles from Earth, Webb observes without thermal noise.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:28.000
From exoplanet atmospheres to stellar nurseries shrouded in dust, infrared optics open a new era.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:50.000
This is only the first calibration frame of a decade-long survey into how our universe took shape.
